# Web

Browser fetch wrappers — a thin, typed layer over the correlated `fetchSend`
core. Every wrapper returns the REQUEST ID that keys the whole response
surface: when the response (or a structured transport failure) arrives, the
completion closure runs as `onDone(reqId)` and reads its own request's state
via `responseStatus(reqId)`, `responseBody(reqId)`, `responseHeader(reqId,
name)`, and `responseError(reqId)` — never a shared global, so concurrent
requests cannot misdeliver. `fetchAbort(reqId)` and `fetchSetTimeout(reqId,
millis)` control an in-flight request by the same id.

## Note
GET `url`. The least-ceremony read: no body, no headers.

## To webGet (url: Text) and (onDone: fn(Int) -> Unit) -> Int:
    Let headers be a new Seq of Text.
    Return fetchSend("GET", url, "", headers, onDone).

## Note
POST a JSON `body` to `url` with `content-type: application/json`.

## To postJson (url: Text) and (body: Text) and (onDone: fn(Int) -> Unit) -> Int:
    Let headers be a new Seq of Text.
    Push "content-type" to headers.
    Push "application/json" to headers.
    Return fetchSend("POST", url, body, headers, onDone).

## Note
PUT a JSON `body` to `url` with `content-type: application/json`.

## To putJson (url: Text) and (body: Text) and (onDone: fn(Int) -> Unit) -> Int:
    Let headers be a new Seq of Text.
    Push "content-type" to headers.
    Push "application/json" to headers.
    Return fetchSend("PUT", url, body, headers, onDone).

## Note
DELETE the resource at `url`.

## To deleteAt (url: Text) and (onDone: fn(Int) -> Unit) -> Int:
    Let headers be a new Seq of Text.
    Return fetchSend("DELETE", url, "", headers, onDone).

## Note
POSTs a JSON `body` with an `idempotency-key` header, so a retried request can never be applied twice server-side.

A retry may follow a timeout or a network failure. There is NO implicit retry — retrying is
the caller's decision, made safe by the key.
## To postJsonIdempotent (url: Text) and (body: Text) and (idempotencyKey: Text) and (onDone: fn(Int) -> Unit) -> Int:
    Let headers be a new Seq of Text.
    Push "content-type" to headers.
    Push "application/json" to headers.
    Push "idempotency-key" to headers.
    Push idempotencyKey to headers.
    Return fetchSend("POST", url, body, headers, onDone).

## Note
PUTs a JSON `body` conditioned on `x-expected-version`, so the write fails if the resource has moved on.

The server rejects a stale write (typically `409`) — the optimistic-concurrency
companion to `postJsonIdempotent`.
## To putJsonExpecting (url: Text) and (body: Text) and (expectedVersion: Text) and (onDone: fn(Int) -> Unit) -> Int:
    Let headers be a new Seq of Text.
    Push "content-type" to headers.
    Push "application/json" to headers.
    Push "x-expected-version" to headers.
    Push expectedVersion to headers.
    Return fetchSend("PUT", url, body, headers, onDone).

## Note
Does one route-pattern segment accept one path segment? A `:name` segment accepts
any value; every other segment must be exactly equal.

## To segmentMatches (seg: Text) and (val: Text) -> Bool:
    If startsWith(seg, ":"):
        Return true.
    Return seg is equal to val.

## Note
`current` — an element's `class` attribute — with `c` added, or unchanged when it is
already there.

A class list is SPACE-SEPARATED WORDS, so membership is a WORD test and never a
substring one: `"active"` contains `"act"`, and a page that checked for a substring
could never add `act` to an element already carrying `active`, nor remove one
without cutting the other in half.

`classListWith("a b", "c")` is `"a b c"`; `classListWith("a b", "b")` is `"a b"`.
## To classListWith (current: Text, c: Text) -> Text:
    Let parts be split(current, " ").
    Let n be length of parts.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is at most n:
        If (item i of parts) is equal to c:
            Return current.
        Set i to i + 1.
    If current is equal to "":
        Return c.
    Let mutable out be current.
    Set out to out combined with " ".
    Set out to out combined with c.
    Return out.

## Note
`current` with `c` gone and every other class left alone.

Empty pieces are dropped, so removing a class cannot leave a double space or a leading one
behind — a `class=" a"` reads the same to a browser and differently to every test that compares
rendered markup.

`classListWithout("a b c", "b")` is `"a c"`; removing one that is not there changes
nothing.
## To classListWithout (current: Text, c: Text) -> Text:
    Let parts be split(current, " ").
    Let n be length of parts.
    Let mutable kept be a new Seq of Text.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is at most n:
        Let w be item i of parts.
        If w is not equal to c:
            If w is not equal to "":
                Push w to kept.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return join(kept, " ").

## Note
`t` with the four characters that mean something to an HTML parser replaced by
their entities — the escape a server-rendered page puts around every hole.

A hole carries data the SERVER did not write: a path segment, a database row, a
form field. Unescaped, a visitor who asks for the right URL runs script in every
other visitor's browser. `&` goes first, or the entities this adds would themselves
be re-escaped; `"` is included because the same function guards attribute values,
where a bare quote ends the attribute and everything after it becomes markup
nobody wrote.

`htmlEscape("<b>")` is `"&lt;b&gt;"`; `htmlEscape("a\"b")` is `"a&quot;b"`.
## To htmlEscape (t: Text) -> Text:
    Let mutable out be "".
    For each c in t:
        Let mutable piece be c.
        If c is equal to "&":
            Set piece to "&amp;".
        If c is equal to "<":
            Set piece to "&lt;".
        If c is equal to ">":
            Set piece to "&gt;".
        If c is equal to chr(34):
            Set piece to "&quot;".
        Set out to out + piece.
    Return out.

## Note
The same for TEXT content, where a quote is an ordinary character.

Three characters, not four. A `"` between tags is just a quote; only inside an attribute value,
where a quote is what ENDS the value, does it have to go. Escaping it anyway is harmless to read
and not harmless to compare: the served bytes stopped matching the painted ones for any hole
holding one, and every cross-tier check in this codebase compares bytes.

## To htmlEscapeText (t: Text) -> Text:
    Let mutable out be "".
    For each c in t:
        Let mutable piece be c.
        If c is equal to "&":
            Set piece to "&amp;".
        If c is equal to "<":
            Set piece to "&lt;".
        If c is equal to ">":
            Set piece to "&gt;".
        Set out to out + piece.
    Return out.

## Note
Whether `t` reads as a whole number — the check a router runs BEFORE binding a
`(id: Int)` path parameter.

`parseInt` is strict and TRAPS on text that is not a number, which is right for a
program reading its own data and wrong for a server reading a stranger's URL: a
request for `/todos/abc` must be answered `400`, not kill the process. So the router
asks first. Rejecting `""` and a lone `"-"` matters as much as rejecting `"abc"` —
both would otherwise reach `parseInt`.

`isIntText("42")` and `isIntText("-5")` are true; `""`, `"-"`, `"4a"` and `"abc"` are false.
## To isIntText (t: Text) -> Bool:
    If t is equal to "":
        Return false.
    If t is equal to "-":
        Return false.
    Let mutable ok be true.
    Let mutable i be 0.
    For each c in t:
        Set i to i + 1.
        If contains("0123456789", c) is equal to false:
            If i is equal to 1 and c is equal to "-":
                Set ok to ok.
            Otherwise:
                Set ok to false.
    Return ok.

## Note
Does `pattern` match `path`? A `:name` segment matches any one segment, every other
segment must be equal, and the counts must agree.

## To matchRoute (pattern: Text) and (path: Text) -> Bool:
    Let ps be split(pattern, "/").
    Let xs be split(path, "/").
    Let np be length of ps.
    Let nx be length of xs.
    If np is equal to nx:
        Let mutable i be 1.
        While i is at most np:
            Let seg be item i of ps.
            Let val be item i of xs.
            If segmentMatches(seg, val) is equal to false:
                Return false.
            Set i to i + 1.
        Return true.
    Return false.

## Note
Extract the value of the `:name` segment of `pattern` from `path` — the safe path parameter.

Returns "" when `pattern` does not match `path`, or names a segment the pattern
has no `:name` slot for, so a caller never reads a stale or wrong id.
`routeParam("/todos/:id", "id", "/todos/42")` is "42".
## To routeParam (pattern: Text) and (name: Text) and (path: Text) -> Text:
    If matchRoute(pattern, path) is equal to false:
        Return "".
    Let ps be split(pattern, "/").
    Let xs be split(path, "/").
    Let np be length of ps.
    Let key be ":" combined with name.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is at most np:
        Let seg be item i of ps.
        If seg is equal to key:
            Return item i of xs.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return "".


## Note
The PATH half of a URL — everything before the first `?`.

A router matches on the path, and a query string is decoration a client may add to
any URL: a `get` form navigates to `action?field=value`, a share link carries a
`?ref=`, an analytics tool appends `?utm_source=`. Comparing the whole URL against
a route's path 404s every one of them.
`pathOf("/search?q=hi")` is `"/search"`; `pathOf("/search")` is `"/search"`.

## To pathOf (url: Text) -> Text:
    Let parts be split(url, "?").
    Return item 1 of parts.

## Note
The QUERY half of a URL — everything after the first `?`, or `""` when there is none.

Only the FIRST `?` separates; the rest are ordinary characters of the query, so a
value carrying one survives rather than being truncated at it.
`queryString("/search?q=hi")` is `"q=hi"`; `queryString("/search?")` is `""`.

## To queryString (url: Text) -> Text:
    Let parts be split(url, "?").
    Let n be length of parts.
    If n is at most 1:
        Return "".
    Let mutable q be item 2 of parts.
    Let mutable i be 3.
    While i is at most n:
        Let piece be item i of parts.
        Set q to q + "?" + piece.
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return q.

## Note
The value of a hex digit byte, or 99 — a value no nibble can hold — when it is not one.

## To hexDigitValue (b: Int) -> Int:
    If b is greater than 47 and b is less than 58:
        Return b - 48.
    If b is greater than 96 and b is less than 103:
        Return b - 87.
    If b is greater than 64 and b is less than 71:
        Return b - 55.
    Return 99.

## Note
Undo the form encoding a browser applies to a query string or a posted form body:
`+` is a space and `%XX` is a byte.

Decoded over BYTES rather than characters, because percent-encoding encodes bytes —
a multi-byte character arrives as several escapes (`%E2%9C%93` is one check mark, not
three characters), and decoding each escape to a character of its own would produce
mojibake the sender never wrote.

A `%` that is not followed by two hex digits is left ALONE rather than dropped or
guessed at: it is a literal percent sign in text somebody typed, and a decoder that
swallowed it would quietly change their data.
`urlDecode("a+b")` is `"a b"`; `urlDecode("%2F")` is `"/"`; `urlDecode("50%")` is `"50%"`.

## To urlDecode (t: Text) -> Text:
    Let sb be text_bytes(t).
    Let n be length of sb.
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Int.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is at most n:
        Let b be item i of sb.
        Let mutable step be 1.
        If b is equal to 43:
            Push 32 to out.
        Otherwise:
            Let mutable decoded be false.
            If b is equal to 37 and i + 2 is at most n:
                Let hp be i + 1.
                Let lp be i + 2.
                Let hi be hexDigitValue(item hp of sb).
                Let lo be hexDigitValue(item lp of sb).
                If hi is less than 16 and lo is less than 16:
                    Push hi * 16 + lo to out.
                    Set step to 3.
                    Set decoded to true.
            If decoded is equal to false:
                Push b to out.
        Set i to i + step.
    Return text_from_bytes(out).

## Note
The value of query parameter `name` in a query STRING, decoded, or `""` when absent.

A query is a list of `&`-separated pairs, so a reader that understood only the first
would hand a route someone else's value the moment a form had two fields. The value
is rejoined at `=` before decoding, because `=` is legal INSIDE a value (base64
padding ends in one) and splitting it away would truncate the data.
`queryParam("q=logos&page=3", "page")` is `"3"`.

## To queryParam (query: Text) and (name: Text) -> Text:
    Let pairs be split(query, "&").
    Let n be length of pairs.
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is at most n:
        Let pair be item i of pairs.
        Let kv be split(pair, "=").
        Let k be urlDecode(item 1 of kv).
        If k is equal to name:
            Let m be length of kv.
            If m is at most 1:
                Return "".
            Let mutable v be item 2 of kv.
            Let mutable j be 3.
            While j is at most m:
                Let piece be item j of kv.
                Set v to v + "=" + piece.
                Set j to j + 1.
            Return urlDecode(v).
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return "".

## Note
The value of parameter `name` in a request BODY — form-encoded or JSON.

A browser posting a `<form>` sends `a=1&b=2`; a page posting from script sends
JSON. A server that understood only one of those would work from the page and
break from the form, or the reverse, and the failure looks like a missing field
rather than a missing parser. The shape is decided by the first character,
because that is the only thing that distinguishes them.

## To bodyParam (body: Text) and (name: Text) -> Text:
    Let brace be text_from_bytes([123]).
    If startsWith(trim(body), brace):
        Inspect jsonTryParse(body):
            When JOk (doc):
                Let field be jsonGet(doc, name).
                If jsonIsNull(field):
                    Return "".
                Inspect jsonString(field):
                    When Ok (s):
                        Return s.
                    When Err (e):
                        Return jsonSerialize(field).
            When JErr (msg):
                Return "".
    Return queryParam(body, name).

## Note
Percent-encode `t` for a URL or a form body — the inverse of `urlDecode`.

Everything outside the unreserved set (`A-Z a-z 0-9 - . _ ~`) is escaped, INCLUDING
the space: `+` means a space only in a form body, while `%20` means it everywhere,
so the safe encoding is the one that reads the same in both places.
`urlEncode("a b&c")` is `"a%20b%26c"`.

## To urlEncode (t: Text) -> Text:
    Let sb be text_bytes(t).
    Let n be length of sb.
    Let mutable out be "".
    Let mutable i be 1.
    While i is at most n:
        Let b be item i of sb.
        Let unreserved be isUnreservedByte(b).
        If unreserved:
            Set out to out + text_from_bytes([b]).
        Otherwise:
            Set out to out + "%" + hexByteText(b).
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return out.

## Note
Is this byte safe to leave alone in a URL? `A-Z`, `a-z`, `0-9`, `-`, `.`, `_`, `~`.

## To isUnreservedByte (b: Int) -> Bool:
    If b is greater than 47 and b is less than 58:
        Return true.
    If b is greater than 64 and b is less than 91:
        Return true.
    If b is greater than 96 and b is less than 123:
        Return true.
    If b is equal to 45 or b is equal to 46:
        Return true.
    If b is equal to 95 or b is equal to 126:
        Return true.
    Return false.

## Note
One byte as two uppercase hex digits — the `XX` of a `%XX` escape.

## To hexByteText (b: Int) -> Text:
    Let digits be "0123456789ABCDEF".
    Let hi be b // 16.
    Let lo be b % 16.
    Return charAtText(digits, hi + 1) + charAtText(digits, lo + 1).

## Note
The character at 1-based `i` of `s`, or `""` past the end.

## To charAtText (s: Text) and (i: Int) -> Text:
    Let sb be text_bytes(s).
    If i is less than 1:
        Return "".
    If i is greater than length of sb:
        Return "".
    Return text_from_bytes([item i of sb]).

## Note
A multipart boundary that does not occur inside `content`.

★ THE ONE THAT CORRUPTS FILES SILENTLY: if the boundary appears in the content a
server truncates the upload at that byte, and the failure reads as a corrupt file
rather than a framing bug — on some uploads and not others.

DERIVED, never random: a random boundary makes every recorded request
unreproducible and every op-log comparison a coin flip. It starts from a fixed
string and lengthens only when it has to, so the common case is stable and the
awkward case is still safe.

## To multipartBoundary (content: Text) -> Text:
    Let mutable b be "LogosBoundary".
    While contains(content, b):
        Set b to b + "x".
    Return b.

## Note
The `multipart/form-data` body an `Upload` sends — one part, named `file`.

CRLF framing, never bare newlines: a body joined with `\n` looks right in a
terminal and parses as one enormous field.

## To multipartBody (content: Text) -> Text:
    Let b be multipartBoundary(content).
    Let nl be text_from_bytes([13, 10]).
    Let q be chr(34).
    Let head be "--" + b + nl + "Content-Disposition: form-data; name=" + q + "file" + q + nl + nl.
    Return head + content + nl + "--" + b + "--" + nl.

## Note
The `Content-Type` that goes with `multipartBody` — held against it by the boundary
they both derive from the same content, so the header and the body cannot disagree.

## To multipartContentType (content: Text) -> Text:
    Return "multipart/form-data; boundary=" + multipartBoundary(content).

## Note
Neutralise a URL a browser would EXECUTE rather than follow.

A `javascript:` URL in an `href` is script, not a location: a visitor who clicks the
link runs it in the page's own origin. Escaping cannot help, because there is nothing
to escape — `javascript:alert(1)` contains no character `htmlEscape` touches, so it
survives every other guard the page has. The danger is the SCHEME.

`data:` is refused for the same reason: a `data:text/html` document runs in the origin
that opened it, which is the same capability with more steps.

A browser strips leading whitespace and control characters before it reads a scheme, and
compares the scheme case-insensitively — so `JaVaScRiPt:`, a leading space, and a tab
spliced inside the word all reach the same place. This compares the same way, or it would
refuse the plain spelling and pass every disguise of it.

The answer is `about:blank#blocked` rather than `""`: a missing link looks like a styling
mistake and sends the reader to the wrong question, while this is inert, greppable, and
obviously deliberate to anyone who inspects the page.

`safeUrl("/about")` is `"/about"`; `safeUrl("javascript:alert(1)")` is `"about:blank#blocked"`.

## To safeUrl (u: Text) -> Text:
    Let mutable flat be "".
    Repeat for bt in text_bytes(u):
        If bt is greater than 32:
            If bt is not equal to 127:
                Set flat to flat + lower(chr(bt)).
    If startsWith(flat, "javascript:"):
        Return "about:blank#blocked".
    If startsWith(flat, "data:"):
        Return "about:blank#blocked".
    If startsWith(flat, "vbscript:"):
        Return "about:blank#blocked".
    Return u.
